   perspective on the school commute},
Journal = {CIUDAD Y TERRITORIO-ESTUDIOS TERRITORIALES-CYTET},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {58},
Number = {228},
Pages = {768-785},
Month = {SUM},
Abstract = {Active commuting to school and the programs promoting this behavior
   contribute to children's health and autonomy and to more sustainable
   cities. To analyze the predictors of participation in the program in
   Donostia-San Sebastian, including parental fear of crime, 819parents of
   primary school children were surveyed. Using hierarchical logistic
   regression and dominance analysis, the model -accounting for 58\% of
   explained variance- revealed that perceived safety and quality of the
   route, parental mode of transport, and home-to-school distance were the
   most relevant predictors. School type, car availability, children's
   self-efficacy, and neighborhood attachment made smaller contributions,
   while fear of crime was not significant. Implications for practice and
   the intergenerational transmission of active commuting are discussed.},
